"""SEN0401 chapter 8 - leaf concepts, part B: ECDSA, randomness, segwit signing and what changed since the book. Same tuple form as part A."""
__version__ = "1.0.0"
LEAVES_B = [
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4 ecdsa
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "EcdsaMath", "EcdsaFormulas", "s = k^-1 (hash(m) + x R) mod n",
  "ECDSA signs with a random nonce k, takes R as the x coordinate of kG and computes s = k^-1 (hash + x R) modulo n; the verifier recomputes R from s, the hash and the public key.",
  "K.ecdsa_book_formulas()",
  "ECDSA, the Elliptic Curve Digital Signature Algorithm, produces a signature of two numbers, R and s. The signer picks a private nonce k, computes the public nonce K = kG, and takes R as the x coordinate of K; then s = k^-1 (hash(m) + x R) modulo n, where x is the private key and k^-1 is the modular inverse. The verifier computes the point u1 G + u2 X with u1 = hash(m) / s and u2 = R / s, all modulo n, and accepts when its x coordinate equals R. Unlike schnorr, the equation is not linear in the nonce and key, which is why the clean aggregation of the previous section is not available. The chapter implements the formulas exactly as the book gives them: the signature verifies (True), fails for another message hash (False) and its R equals the x coordinate of kG (True), the list [True, False, True] (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (NIST, 2023)."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "EcdsaMath", "SignatureMalleability", "(R, s) and (R, n - s)",
  "For every valid ECDSA signature (R, s) the pair (R, n - s) is also valid for the same message, so a third party can change a signature without knowing the key.",
  "K.malleability()",
  "A signature scheme is malleable if someone other than the signer can turn a valid signature into a different valid signature for the same message. ECDSA is: if (R, s) verifies, then (R, n - s) verifies too, because replacing s by its negative flips the sign of the point u1 G + u2 X but leaves its x coordinate unchanged. The chapter's check gives [True, True, True]: the original verifies, the negated one verifies, and exactly one of the two is the 'low' value at most n/2. Before segwit a changed signature changed the transaction identifier, because the signature was inside the transaction that is hashed to the txid, which BIP146 describes as the ability of any relay node to transform the signature without the key, and BIP146 made the high form non-standard (Lau and Wuille, 2016) (Wuille et al., 2020)."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "EcdsaMath", "LowSRule", "always the smaller of s and n - s",
  "The chapter's signer always returns the low s, so every signature it makes passes the strict DER and low-S rules, with lengths 70 or 71 bytes.",
  "K.lib_signature_low_s_and_strict()",
  "To remove the malleability, a signer chooses between s and n - s the one that is at most n/2, which is called the low-S rule. Bitcoin Core's verification function normalizes the received s and its policy rejects the high form. The chapter's signing function follows the rule: of 200 signatures it makes for 200 different keys and messages, all pass the strict DER check ported from Core (True), all have a low s (True), and their DER lengths are 70 and 71 bytes without the hash type byte, [True, True, [70, 71]], the two lengths that depend on whether the random-looking R happens to have its top bit set (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "DerEncoding", "DerLayout", "3045 0221 00 884d... 0220 4b9f... 01",
  "A DER-encoded ECDSA signature is a sequence of two integers, tagged 0x30, with a length byte, followed in Bitcoin by the hash type byte; the book's sample has nine elements.",
  "K.book_der_sample()",
  "DER, the Distinguished Encoding Rules, is a standard way to write structured data as bytes, and Bitcoin used it for ECDSA signatures from the start. The book's sample is 3045 0221 00884d...81 0220 4b9f...13 01. The elements, as the chapter's parser finds, are: 0x30 (a sequence follows), 0x45 (its length, 69 bytes), 0x02 (an integer follows), 0x21 (length 33), the integer R with a leading 0x00 because its top bit is set and DER integers are signed, 0x02, 0x20 (length 32), the integer S, and the Bitcoin hash type byte 0x01, nine elements in all. The whole is 72 bytes, the string passes Bitcoin Core's strict DER check, and the result [72, 69, '30', '45', '21', '20', '01', 9, True] confirms the book (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Wuille, 2015)."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "DerEncoding", "BookSampleOnChain", "block 277,316",
  "The signature in the book's DER section is the real signature of a main-chain transaction mined on 27 December 2013.",
  "K.book_sample_in_mainnet_tx()",
  "The long hexadecimal string that the book dissects was not made up for the book: the chapter finds it, byte for byte, in the saved record of the transaction 0627052b6f28912f2703066a912ea577f2ce4da4caa5a5fbd8a57286c345c2f2 from the mempool.space block explorer. The record says the transaction is in block 277,316 with the block time 1388185914, that is 2013-12-27 23:11:54 UTC. The result [True, 277316, 1388185914] shows that the signature is in the input's scriptSig (the unlocking script of chapter 7) and where it is on the chain (Mempool Space, 2026) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "DerEncoding", "BookSampleVerifies", "the signature, the key and the transaction agree",
  "The book's sample verifies against the legacy signature hash of its transaction and the 65-byte public key in the same input, and fails if the hash type is changed.",
  "K.book_sample_verifies()",
  "Because the sample is a real signature, the chapter checks it as a node would: it takes the input's 65-byte uncompressed public key, rebuilds the legacy signature hash of the transaction with the previous output's script and the hash type 01 (SIGHASH_ALL), and verifies. The result is [65, 1, True, False, 72]: the key has 65 bytes, the hash type is 1, the signature verifies, it does not verify when the hash is computed with hash type 2 (NONE), and the signature with its flag byte is 72 bytes. This shows the machinery of the chapter on a live example from the chain (Mempool Space, 2026) (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "DerEncoding", "SampleHighR", "R begins with 0x88",
  "The sample's R has its top bit set, which costs a padding zero and one extra byte, while its s is the low form.",
  "K.book_sample_s_low()",
  "In DER an integer is signed, so a 256-bit number whose top bit is set needs a leading 0x00 byte to stay positive, which is why the sample's R takes 33 bytes. The chapter finds for the sample that s is at most n/2 (True), that R is at least 2 to the 255 (True) and that R has 64 hexadecimal digits, that is 32 bytes without the padding. About half of all random R values have the top bit set, so about half of the older Bitcoin signatures were 72 bytes with the flag, and Bitcoin Core now avoids this by trying nonces until R is small, as the randomness section below shows (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "ConsensusAndPolicy", "PolicyVerdicts", "what the node's mempool says",
  "Bitcoin Core 31.1 accepts a normal signature into its mempool and rejects a high-S signature, a non-minimal DER encoding and a signature of another message with a specific error each.",
  "K.core_policy_verdicts()",
  "A node applies stricter rules to the transactions it relays than the consensus rules that decide whether a block is valid. The chapter has Bitcoin Core 31.1 test four versions of the same spend, made with the chapter's own key on the private test network: the ordinary low-S signature is allowed; the high-S version is refused with 'Non-canonical signature: S value is unnecessarily high'; a version with a non-minimal DER padding is refused with 'Non-canonical DER signature'; and a signature of another message is refused because, for a failed check, the signature must be the empty value ('Signature must be zero for failed CHECK(MULTI)SIG operation'). Each verdict is read from the node's testmempoolaccept answer (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "ConsensusAndPolicy", "StrictDerConsensus", "mined in a block or not",
  "A high-S signature is valid in a block, because low-S is only policy, while a signature that is not strict DER makes the block invalid, because strict DER is a consensus rule.",
  "K.core_high_s_consensus()",
  "The difference between policy and consensus can be tested by asking the node to put a transaction directly into a block, which skips the relay rules. In the regtest run the transaction with the high-S signature was accepted in a block (True), the one with a non-DER signature was not (False), and the error of the second contains 'Non-canonical DER signature' (True): [True, False, True]. The second rule is a consensus rule since BIP66 was activated by a soft fork in July 2015, when its 95 percent threshold was reached on 4 July, and Pieter Wuille later disclosed (28 July 2015) that a specially crafted transaction could have forked the blockchain between nodes with different signature parsers until BIP66 made it invalid (Wuille, 2015); the first is a relay rule only. The book, which does not separate them, is right that both kinds of signature are 'wrong' but wrong in different ways (Wuille, 2015) (Lau and Wuille, 2016)."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "ConsensusAndPolicy", "PolicyFlags", "LOW_S, NULLFAIL, STRICTENC, DERSIG",
  "Bitcoin Core's standard policy flags include the low-S, null-fail, strict encoding and DER signature checks.",
  "K.verify_flags_in_policy()",
  "In Bitcoin Core, the extra checks that a standard transaction must pass are written as script verification flags in src/policy/policy.h. The chapter reads the saved copy and finds all four named flags in it: SCRIPT_VERIFY_LOW_S (the smaller of s and n - s), SCRIPT_VERIFY_NULLFAIL (a failed check must have an empty signature), SCRIPT_VERIFY_STRICTENC (strict encoding of keys and flags) and SCRIPT_VERIFY_DERSIG (strict DER, which is also in consensus), [True, True, True, True] (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("EcdsaSignatures", "ConsensusAndPolicy", "LegacySigopsCap", "2,500 in policy since Core 30",
  "Bitcoin Core 30.0 allows at most 2,500 potentially executed legacy signature operations in a standard transaction, the limit that BIP54 would make a consensus rule.",
  "K.sigops_limit_in_core_and_bip54()",
  "A signature operation is a use of a checking opcode such as OP_CHECKSIG, and in the legacy format each one makes the node hash a large part of the transaction. Bitcoin Core's policy header defines MAX_TX_LEGACY_SIGOPS as 2500 and BIP54, the consensus cleanup (status Complete), makes a transaction with strictly more than 2500 such operations invalid. The result is [2500, True]: the number in the source and the text in the BIP. Sergio Demian Lerner reported on 30 January 2013 'a transaction that takes at least 3 minutes to verify', recorded as CVE-2013-2292 (Bitcointalk, 2013). The release notes of Core 30.0 say the limit prepares for a possible BIP54 deployment, and the BIP states that the limit reduces the worst-case block validation time by a factor of 40 (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Poinsot and Corallo, 2025)."),
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5 randomness
 ("Randomness", "NonceSafety", "NonceReuse", "two payments, one k",
  "If two ECDSA signatures from the same key use the same nonce k, anyone can compute k and then the private key from the two signatures.",
  "K.nonce_reuse()",
  "The nonce k is a one-time secret, and the book's warning is that using it twice destroys the key. The reason is algebra. Two signatures on different hashes h1 and h2 with the same k have the same R, and s1 - s2 = k^-1 (h1 - h2), so k = (h1 - h2) / (s1 - s2); then from s1 = k^-1 (h1 + x R) the private key is x = (s1 k - h1) / R, all modulo n. The chapter signs two different payments with one nonce and recovers the key: the two R values are equal (True), the recovered key equals the private key (True) and the recovered nonce equals k (True), [True, True, True]. This is the failure mode in the Android story of 2013 (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023) (Breitner and Heninger, 2019)."),
 ("Randomness", "NonceSafety", "DistinctNonces", "the same formula with two different nonces",
  "With two different nonces the same formula returns a number that is not the key, so the attack needs the repetition.",
  "K.nonce_not_reused_is_safe()",
  "To show that the repetition is what the attack uses, the chapter applies the same recovery formula to two signatures that use different nonces and finds that the number it returns is not the private key, the answer False for 'equals the key'. A biased generator is also dangerous: Breitner and Heninger (2019) computed hundreds of Bitcoin private keys and dozens of Ethereum, Ripple, SSH and HTTPS private keys by attacking signatures in public blockchains and Internet-wide scans, and their method needs only that the nonce is not generated uniformly at random, which is why the standards ask for nonces that are random or derived from a hash of the secret and the message (Breitner and Heninger, 2019) (Pornin, 2013)."),
 ("Randomness", "DeterministicNonces", "Rfc6979Vector", "the published P-256 test vector",
  "The chapter's implementation of the nonce derivation of RFC 6979 reproduces the nonce printed in the RFC's appendix for the curve P-256.",
  "K.rfc6979_p256_vector()",
  "RFC 6979 (Pornin, 2013) specifies a way to derive the nonce k from the private key and the message hash with HMAC, a keyed hash, so that k is unpredictable to an attacker yet never repeats for different messages. The chapter implements the derivation and checks it against the vector in the RFC's appendix A.2.5 (the curve P-256, the message 'sample', SHA-256), read back from the saved text of the RFC: the derived k equals the published one, and its first hexadecimal digits are 0xa6e3c57d, [True, '0xa6e3c57d']. The same derivation works for secp256k1, which is where Bitcoin uses it (Pornin, 2013)."),
 ("Randomness", "DeterministicNonces", "DeterministicSigning", "same message, same signature",
  "The chapter's ECDSA signer is deterministic: the same key and message give the same signature, another message gives another.",
  "K.rfc6979_deterministic()",
  "A deterministic signer has the practical advantage that a test can compare signatures byte for byte, and the safety advantage that no random number is needed at signing time. The chapter signs the same message twice and gets identical signatures (True) and signs another message and gets a different one (True), [True, True]. The book recommends BIP340, RFC 6979 or a similar method and adds that extra entropy may be mixed in as protection against side-channel and fault attacks (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("Randomness", "DeterministicNonces", "LibraryNonceMatches", "the same nonce as the standard gives",
  "The signature the chapter's signer makes is the one obtained from RFC 6979's nonce and the plain ECDSA formulas, normalized to low s.",
  "K.rfc6979_matches_library_nonce()",
  "To check that the signer does what it is said to do, the chapter derives the nonce with its own RFC 6979 function, signs with that nonce using the plain formulas of the book and compares the DER-encoded result with the signature produced by the library's signing function: they are equal (True), once the high s has been replaced by its negative. The library's signature therefore has no hidden randomness; it is a function of the key and the message (Pornin, 2013) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("Randomness", "DeterministicNonces", "CoreDeterminism", "regtest signs twice",
  "Bitcoin Core 31.1 gives the same signature when it signs the same transaction twice, for ECDSA and for taproot, because it derives the nonce deterministically and uses zero auxiliary data for schnorr.",
  "K.core_ecdsa_deterministic_and_schnorr_aux_zero()",
  "The regtest run signs the same unsigned transaction twice with the same wallet, once for a bech32 (ECDSA) input and once for a bech32m (schnorr) input, and compares the signed bytes. Both pairs are equal, [True, True]. In Bitcoin Core's source the ECDSA signing function calls the RFC 6979 nonce function, and the code that signs a taproot input contains the comment 'Use uint256{} as aux_rnd for now', so the auxiliary random data of BIP340 is the zero value; the book says only that BIP340 and RFC 6979 can be deterministic, so the actual setting of the reference software is a point it leaves out (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("Randomness", "SignatureSize", "LowRGrinding", "300 signatures from one transaction",
  "Bitcoin Core signs repeatedly until R is small, so all 300 signatures in a transaction with 300 inputs are 70 or 71 bytes with the hash type and none is longer.",
  "K.core_signature_lengths()",
  "Because DER needs a padding byte whenever R's top bit is set, Bitcoin Core tries different nonces (its source says 'Grind for low R') until R is below 2 to the 255 and the signature is shorter by one byte. The chapter has the node sign a transaction with 300 inputs and finds the lengths of the 300 signatures with the hash type byte: 298 of 71 bytes and 2 of 70 bytes, summarized as [300, 71, 70, 300] (inputs, longest, shortest, count). All 300 verify with the chapter's code ([300, 300]) and all 300 have a low R ([300, 300]) (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("Randomness", "SignatureSize", "LibraryHighR", "about half have the top bit set",
  "A signer that does not grind produces a high R in about half of its signatures: of 400 signatures made by the chapter's library, 197 had the top bit of R set.",
  "K.library_signatures_high_r_share()",
  "The grinding of Bitcoin Core is not a quirk: it is needed because a signer that does not grind produces 72-byte signatures half of the time. The chapter's own deterministic signer, which does not grind, made 400 signatures for 400 keys and messages, 197 of which had R at least 2 to the 255, which is between 40 and 60 percent of 400 (True): [400, 197, True]. Draft BIP 461 proposes to make the low-R grinding part of a standard deterministic ECDSA signature, so that signatures are at most 70 bytes when DER encoded (Gilligan, 2026)."),
 ("Randomness", "SignatureSize", "GrindingSource", "the lines of Bitcoin Core",
  "Bitcoin Core's key.cpp has the low-R grind and the RFC 6979 function, and sign.cpp has the zero auxiliary value and the refusal to produce the SINGLE bug signature.",
  "K.core_grind_and_aux()",
  "The behaviours reported in the previous leaves can be found in the source of the software at the commit the course pins. The saved key.cpp contains the comment 'Grind for low R' and the name secp256k1_nonce_function_rfc6979; the saved sign.cpp contains 'Use uint256{} as aux_rnd for now.' and the comment about the SIGHASH_SINGLE bug that ends 'So don't produce it.' The four tests return [True, True, True, True]. Reading the source does not replace the runs; the runs show that the released binary does what the source says (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6 segwit signing
 ("SegwitSigning", "HashingCost", "QuadraticHashing", "bytes hashed for 1, 10, 100, 1000 inputs",
  "In the legacy algorithm every signature check hashes nearly the whole transaction, so the bytes hashed grow with the square of the number of inputs; BIP143 hashes each part once.",
  "K.hashing_bytes()",
  "Each of the n inputs of a legacy transaction needs a signature check, and each check hashes a copy of the transaction that is about n inputs long, so the total work is about n squared. The chapter counts the bytes passed to SHA-256 for transactions with 1, 10, 100 and 1,000 inputs and one output: for the legacy algorithm 146, 5,150, 420,500 and 41,107,000 bytes, and for the BIP143 algorithm 384, 2,670, 25,530 and 254,130. BIP143 hashes the shared parts once and reuses them, so the work is proportional to n: the second column is [[1, 146, 384], [10, 5150, 2670], [100, 420500, 25530], [1000, 41107000, 254130]]. For a single input the new algorithm hashes more bytes, but for large transactions it is far cheaper (Lau and Wuille, 2016) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SegwitSigning", "HashingCost", "HashingGrowth", "10 times the inputs",
  "Going from 100 to 1,000 inputs multiplies the legacy work by 97.8 and the segwit work by 10.0.",
  "K.hashing_growth()",
  "The book says the hash operations of the legacy algorithm increase 'roughly quadratically' and those of BIP143 'in O(n)'. The two growth factors from 100 to 1,000 inputs are [97.8, 10.0]: ten times the inputs cost about ninety-eight times the hashing in the legacy algorithm, which is almost the hundredfold of a perfect square law, and exactly ten times in the BIP143 algorithm. BIP143 itself says a 1 MB transaction with 5,569 signature operations may take 25 seconds to verify under the old algorithm (Lau and Wuille, 2016)."),
 ("SegwitSigning", "ValueCommitment", "AmountCommitment", "a segwit signature covers the amount",
  "In BIP143 the signature hash includes the amount of the coin being spent, so a signer cannot be tricked about the amount, which the legacy hash does not offer.",
  "K.bip143_commits_to_amount()",
  "In the legacy algorithm the signer sees the previous output's script but not its value, so a signer who does not have the previous transaction cannot know from the signature hash how much the coin is worth and therefore how large the fee is. BIP143 states that every signature hash type commits to the amount being spent, and puts the amount of the input into the hash, and the chapter shows that changing the amount by one satoshi changes the digest (True). The book's segwit section does not mention this, and a note in its source says that the coverage of values to help hardware wallets still has to be written (Lau and Wuille, 2016) (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SegwitSigning", "ValueCommitment", "TaprootAmounts", "all spent amounts, unless ANYONECANPAY",
  "A taproot signature commits to the amounts of all inputs being spent, unless ANYONECANPAY is used, when it commits only to its own.",
  "K.bip341_commits_to_all_spent_amounts()",
  "BIP341 extends the idea: the signature message includes the amounts and the scripts of all the outputs being spent, which is the kind of coverage the editor's note in the book's source hopes will help hardware wallets. The chapter changes the amount of the second input and finds that the digest of the first input changes under the default hash type (True), and does not change under ANYONECANPAY (True): [True, True]. The consequence is that taproot spenders who use the common default or SIGHASH_ALL commit to the whole picture of the transaction (Wuille et al., 2020)."),
 ("SegwitSigning", "TaprootSigning", "FirstTaprootSpend", "block 709,635 on 14 November 2021",
  "The taproot key-path spend recorded as the first on the main chain has a one-element witness holding a 64-byte schnorr signature, which verifies with the chapter's code.",
  "K.first_taproot_spend()",
  "The taproot soft fork took effect at block 709,632 on 14 November 2021, and the transaction 33e794d0... is recorded as the first spend of a taproot output through the key path; this package did not check that no earlier one exists. The chapter checks the saved record of that transaction and finds the block height 709,635, one item in the witness, a signature of 64 bytes (the default hash type), and that the schnorr signature verifies against the key of the spent output with the BIP341 signature message: [709635, 1, 64, True]. The record gives its time as 2021-11-14 05:40:13 UTC (Mempool Space, 2026) (Wuille et al., 2020)."),
 ("SegwitSigning", "TaprootSigning", "TaprootVerdicts", "what the node says about five malformed signatures",
  "Bitcoin Core 31.1 accepts the 64-byte default and the explicit ALL signature and rejects an explicit zero hash type, a 63-byte signature and two altered signatures, each with its own message.",
  "K.core_taproot_verdicts()",
  "On the private test network, the chapter signs a taproot input with its own key and tests six variants of the signature with testmempoolaccept: the 64-byte default is allowed, and so is the 65-byte signature with the explicit hash type 0x01; the 65-byte signature with the explicit value 0x00 is refused with 'Invalid Schnorr signature hash type'; the 63-byte one with 'Invalid Schnorr signature size'; a signature with one flipped bit and a signature whose hash type does not match the one it was made with are refused with 'Invalid Schnorr signature'. The book says an explicit SIGHASH_ALL wastes a byte; it does not say that an explicit 0x00 is not allowed (Wuille et al., 2020) (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7 since the book
 ("SinceTheBook", "StandardsStatus", "BipStatus", "the status of thirteen BIPs",
  "In the BIP index, 66, 143, 327, 340, 341 and 342 are Deployed, 146 is Closed, 373 and 54 are Complete, and 118, 390, 461 and 361 are Drafts.",
  "K.bips_status()",
  "A BIP, a Bitcoin Improvement Proposal, has a status that says how far it has gone. Reading the index of the BIPs repository at the pinned commit, the chapter finds: 66 (strict DER), 143 (segwit signing), 327 (MuSig2), 340, 341 and 342 (schnorr, taproot and tapscript) Deployed; 146 (signature malleability) Closed; 373 (MuSig2 fields of PSBT) and 54 (consensus cleanup) Complete; and 118 (ANYPREVOUT), 390 (the musig descriptor), 461 (deterministic ECDSA) and 361 (post-quantum sunset) Draft. A Draft is a proposal that anyone may change or that may never be deployed, so it must not be described as part of Bitcoin (Bitcoin BIPs, 2026) (Wuille et al., 2020)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "StandardsStatus", "CoreMusigSupport", "key aggregation in 30.0, signing in 31.0",
  "Bitcoin Core 30.0 added the musig() descriptor and the MuSig2 fields of PSBT, 31.0 added signing, and the descriptor documentation of 29.0 does not have it.",
  "K.core_musig_support()",
  "A descriptor is Bitcoin Core's text format for describing which keys and scripts make up addresses. The saved documentation of Core's list of implemented BIPs says that BIP327 key aggregation through the musig() descriptor is supported as of v30.0 and signing as of v31.0, and the saved descriptor documentation shows that the version 29.0 file has tr(KEY) but no mention of musig, while the version 31.0 file does. The five checks return [True, True, True, True, True]. For a student this means that the scriptless multisignature of the book can now be created with a standard wallet command (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Chow, 2024)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "StandardsStatus", "CoreBipList", "which BIPs Core lists",
  "Bitcoin Core's list of implemented BIPs has 66, 143, 327, 340, 373 and 390 and does not have the drafts 118, 461 and 361 or BIP 54.",
  "K.core_doc_lists_bips()",
  "The list in Bitcoin Core's doc/bips.md is the project's own answer to which proposals it implements. Reading the saved copy at the pinned commit, the BIPs 66, 143, 327, 340, 373 and 390 appear, and 118, 54, 461 and 361 do not: {66: True, 143: True, 327: True, 340: True, 373: True, 390: True, 118: False, 54: False, 461: False, 361: False}. The absence of BIP 54 fits its status: Core 30.0 prepared the ground with the 2,500-operation relay limit, but the consensus change is not deployed (Bitcoin Core, 2026) (Poinsot and Corallo, 2025)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "StandardsStatus", "ThresholdBipGap", "no threshold BIP in the index",
  "The BIP index has no entry for a threshold signature scheme, while Optech lists a 2024 mention of a BIP proposed for FROST.",
  "K.no_threshold_bip_in_index()",
  "The book states that no scriptless threshold signature protocol had been proposed as a BIP. The chapter looks in the saved index of the BIPs repository for the word FROST (not found, False) and for the word threshold (found once, in the title of an unrelated soft-fork activation proposal, 'Reduced threshold Segwit MASF', True), and in the saved Optech topic page finds the 2024 mention 'BIP proposed for creating FROST scriptless threshold signatures for Bitcoin' (True): [False, 1, True, True]. So the sentence of the book is a snapshot of 2023 and a proposal exists but has no number in the index. FROST itself is a published threshold scheme (Komlo and Goldberg, 2021) (Bitcoin Optech, 2026)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "LibrarySupport", "LibsecpChanges", "0.8.0, 3 August 2026",
  "The secp256k1 library removed its deprecated function alias for schnorr signing in version 0.8.0, and Bitcoin Core calls the function with the name secp256k1_schnorrsig_sign32.",
  "K.libsecp_changes()",
  "libsecp256k1 is the C library that Bitcoin Core uses for every signature and key operation described in this chapter. Its changelog, saved from the pinned commit, has a version 0.8.0 dated 2026-08-03 (True) that lists 'Removed previously deprecated function alias secp256k1_schnorrsig_sign' (True), and Bitcoin Core's key.cpp calls secp256k1_schnorrsig_sign32 (True): [True, True, True]. The alias was kept for older programs; its removal does not change the signatures but is a reminder that a library's interface is a moving target (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "LibrarySupport", "Core30Sigops", "a limit in the release notes",
  "The release notes of Bitcoin Core 30.0 state the limit of 2,500 legacy signature operations and name BIP54 as the reason.",
  "K.core_release_30_sigops()",
  "The statement is read from the saved release notes of Core 30.0: 'The maximum number of potentially executed legacy signature operations in a single standard transaction is now limited to 2500' and 'The change was done to prepare for a possible BIP54 deployment in the future'. Both sentences are present (True). The limit is policy and not consensus, so a miner can still include a transaction above it, and the cap tells wallet authors what transactions will propagate (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "History", "PatentDates", "17 years from grant or 20 from filing",
  "Schnorr's patent 4,995,082 was filed on 23 February 1990 and published on 19 February 1991, so the statute's greater term ends on 23 February 2010.",
  "K.patent_dates()",
  "For United States applications filed before the cut-off of 1995 that the statute names, a patent lasted the greater of 17 years from grant and 20 years from filing. For the Schnorr patent the dates in the patent record are the filing date 1990-02-23 and the publication date 1991-02-19. The chapter computes 17 years from the grant as 2008-02-19 and 20 years from the filing as 2010-02-23, and finds that the second is later (True); Wikipedia states that the patent expired in February 2010 (Wikipedia, 2026), and the patent record shows the status 'Expired - Lifetime' (True), though the site marks it as an assumption, not a legal conclusion, and the Cornell text of the statute has the rule (True): ['2008-02-19', '2010-02-23', True, True, True]. This is consistent with the book's 'almost two decades' (USPTO, 1991) (Cornell Legal Information Institute, 2026)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "History", "SchnorrToTaprootYears", "from 1991 to 2021",
  "Thirty years separate the publication of Schnorr's journal paper in 1991 from the activation of schnorr signatures in Bitcoin in 2021, and 32 years the Crypto '89 priority.",
  "K.schnorr_to_taproot_years()",
  "The scheme was described by Claus-Peter Schnorr at Crypto '89, published in the proceedings in 1990 and in the Journal of Cryptology in January 1991, and it became part of Bitcoin's rules at block 709,632 on 14 November 2021. The subtraction of years gives [30, 32] (from 1991 and from 1989). Pieter Wuille posted the first public draft of a BIP for 64-byte schnorr signatures to the bitcoin-dev mailing list on 6 July 2018 (Wuille, 2018), and BIP340 was assigned its number on 2020-01-19 (Wuille et al., 2020). Bitcoin began in January 2009, so the activation came 4,698 days after the genesis block, and the choice of ECDSA in 2009 was made at a time when the patent was still in force (Schnorr, 1991) (Schnorr, 1990)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "History", "BookTransactionTime", "27 December 2013, 23:11:54 UTC",
  "The transaction whose signature the book prints was mined on 27 December 2013 at 23:11:54 UTC.",
  "K.timestamp_of_book_tx()",
  "The block time recorded by the explorer for block 277,316 is the Unix time 1388185914, which the chapter converts to the date '2013-12-27 23:11:54' in UTC. A block time is set by the miner, within the limits of the rules, and is not an exact clock, so the sentence is accurate to within minutes or hours rather than seconds (Mempool Space, 2026)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "History", "ActivationGap", "4,698 days after the genesis block",
  "Taproot took effect 4,698 days after the genesis block of 3 January 2009.",
  "K.activation_gap_days()",
  "Counting days from the genesis block on 3 January 2009 to 14 November 2021, the day of the taproot activation at block 709,632, gives 4,698 days, about 12 years and 10 months. It is the length of time during which ECDSA was the only signature algorithm of Bitcoin, which the book states as 'until the activation of the taproot soft fork in 2021' (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "EvidenceTrail", "SourcesRecord", "66 pages fetched, 214 passages found",
  "All 66 sources of this package answered with HTTP 200 and each passage the text relies on was found in the saved copy.",
  "K.sources_record()",
  "The evidence for the chapter is kept in 08-tooling/ch08-evidence: a saved readable copy of each source, a record with the SHA-256 digest of what was received and of what was saved, and the passages checked. The record says [66, 66, 214, 214]: 66 sources, 66 answered with status 200, 214 passages to find and 214 found. The passages are short exact strings from the source, so a reader can search the saved file for them (Antonopoulos and Harding, 2023)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "EvidenceTrail", "CoreNodeRun", "Bitcoin Core 31.1.0 on regtest",
  "The signatures and verdicts of this chapter come from one run of Bitcoin Core 31.1.0 (version number 310100) on a private regtest network.",
  "K.core_node_identity()",
  "Regtest is Bitcoin Core's regression test mode, a private chain on one computer where blocks can be created at will; nothing in it touches the Bitcoin network. The record of the run states the node version 310100, the subversion '/Satoshi:31.1.0/' and the chain 'regtest': [310100, '/Satoshi:31.1.0/', 'regtest']. The numbers come from one run on 15 October 2026 and are stored as a JSON file that the chapter's verification program reads offline, so a reader can repeat the checks without a node (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
 ("SinceTheBook", "EvidenceTrail", "BinaryChecksum", "the SHA-256 of the release archive",
  "The Linux archive of Bitcoin Core 31.1 used for the run has the SHA-256 digest listed in the project's SHA256SUMS file.",
  "K.core_binary_hash()",
  "The release directory's SHA256SUMS file lists b80d9c3e...3f9e for bitcoin-31.1-x86_64-linux-gnu.tar.gz, the archive from which the node was installed; the chapter confirms that the line is in the saved copy of the file and, on the machine where the node was installed, that the archive hashes to it: [True, True]. A checksum shows that the file is the one the project published, though not who published it; the signature check of the release is a separate step that this package does not repeat (Bitcoin Core, 2026)."),
]
